WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:13.617
In 1902 a paleontologist named Barnum Brown found an exciting new fossil.

2
00:00:14.538 --> 00:00:20.522
He found it while he was on a fossil-hunting expedition in Montana, at a site filled with ancient

3
00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:27.727
rocks. Brown was a very well-known and experienced fossil collector who had traveled all over the world hunting

4
00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:28.948
for dinosaur fossils.

5
00:00:30.164 --> 00:00:35.508
And he had just discovered the remains of what is now considered the most famous dinosaur that ever

6
00:00:35.588 --> 00:00:37.969
lived: the Tyrannosaurus rex.

7
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.752
The new fossil was located in solid rock.

8
00:00:42.312 --> 00:00:48.557
In the early days of paleontology, the methods for excavating, or digging out, a fossil were not very

9
00:00:48.597 --> 00:00:53.960
sophisticated. So Brown had to use dynamite to blast the rock into pieces.

10
00:00:54.881 --> 00:01:01.529
Then these broken rock fragments, which weighed hundreds of kilograms, were shipped to the Museum of Natural History

11
00:01:01.669 --> 00:01:02.509
in New York City.

12
00:01:03.749 --> 00:01:09.051
At that time the Museum of Natural History had one of the best dinosaur fossil collections in the

13
00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:16.993
world. Henry Osborn, a museum curator and expert paleontologist there, studied Brown's newly discovered fossils.

14
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:24.515
Osborn wrote the first scientific paper on the new dinosaur, and he also gave it its name: Tyrannosaurus

15
00:01:24.615 --> 00:01:27.136
rex or "tyrant lizard king."

16
00:01:28.316 --> 00:01:30.237
The Tyrannosaurus rex—or "T.

17
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:34.139
rex," as it was soon nicknamed—certainly seemed to deserve its name.

18
00:01:34.879 --> 00:01:39.121
The skeletons Osborn studied revealed a huge and terrifying creature.

19
00:01:39.881 --> 00:01:43.443
In adult form it was 15 meters from head to tail.

20
00:01:43.963 --> 00:01:51.126
It walked on its two back feet and stood almost seven meters high, sometimes weighing over six tons.

21
00:01:51.846 --> 00:01:59.021
With a huge skull, and about sixty long, sharp teeth, the dinosaur could tear off more than two

22
00:01:59.121 --> 00:02:01.922
hundred kilograms of flesh in a single bite!

23
00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:08.325
Osborn also realized that the Tyrannosaurus rex had lived during the late Cretaceous period.

24
00:02:08.805 --> 00:02:15.808
The Cretaceous period began 136 million years ago and ended 65 million years ago.

25
00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:23.332
During this time palm trees, ferns, and flowering plants grew, but grasses had yet to evolve.

26
00:02:24.712 --> 00:02:32.439
Huge flying reptiles called pterosaurs flew overhead while frogs and turtles moved along below in the land and

27
00:02:32.479 --> 00:02:38.665
water. The only mammals in existence at the time were very small, similar in size and shape to

28
00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:39.525
mice or rats.

29
00:02:40.166 --> 00:02:46.792
In this ancient world, the giant Tyrannosaurus rex was probably truly a lizard king!

30
00:02:48.024 --> 00:02:54.526
Six years after his first discovery, Barnum Brown found another Tyrannosaurus rex at the Montana site.

31
00:02:55.546 --> 00:03:01.728
This second skeleton had more bones than the first one, so it was another exciting and useful discovery

32
00:03:01.788 --> 00:03:03.328
for the field of paleontology.

33
00:03:04.128 --> 00:03:06.849
Since Brown's early discoveries, more T.

34
00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:10.130
rex skeletons have been found.

35
00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:17.612
In 1990 paleontologist Susan Hendrickson collected a skeleton that was the biggest and most complete skeleton ever.

36
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:25.287
It was named Sue in Hendrickson's honor, and eventually sold to a Chicago museum for lots of money—about

37
00:03:25.407 --> 00:03:27.089
8.4 million dollars!

38
00:03:27.809 --> 00:03:29.911
In 2000, five more T.

39
00:03:29.972 --> 00:03:34.976
rex skeletons were excavated from the Montana site where Barnum Brown had discovered the first ones.

40
00:03:36.057 --> 00:03:42.484
Some of these newer dinosaurs were very well-preserved, adding more information to experts' growing knowledge.

41
00:03:43.364 --> 00:03:44.905
As a clearer picture of the T.

42
00:03:44.965 --> 00:03:51.148
rex emerged, scientists learned that it had probably reached its full size at around age twenty, as humans

43
00:03:51.188 --> 00:03:58.052
do. Its front legs were short but quite strong, and its back legs had three toes with claws.

44
00:03:59.092 --> 00:03:59.393
The T.

45
00:03:59.453 --> 00:04:08.117
rex probably ate smaller plant-eating dinosaurs, maybe hunting by hiding behind trees, waiting to attack its unsuspecting prey.

46
00:04:09.078 --> 00:04:10.939
With its sharp teeth, the T.

47
00:04:10.999 --> 00:04:12.900
rex could easily rip into food.

48
00:04:13.676 --> 00:04:16.378
But the dinosaur probably didn't just hunt for food.

49
00:04:17.439 --> 00:04:23.803
Instead it likely scavenged food as well, stealing it from other dinosaurs, and eating their leftovers.

50
00:04:25.324 --> 00:04:27.606
Bite marks found on the bones of several T.

51
00:04:27.686 --> 00:04:29.027
rex suggest that the T.

52
00:04:29.107 --> 00:04:31.288
rex may have even dined on other T.

53
00:04:31.368 --> 00:04:32.369
rex dinosaurs!

54
00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.631
Scientists have debated how quickly the T.

55
00:04:35.691 --> 00:04:36.532
rex could move.

56
00:04:37.052 --> 00:04:40.915
They think it probably moved pretty fast—about 16-32 kilometers per hour.

57
00:04:43.052 --> 00:04:48.716
But because of its huge size, the Tyrannosaurus rex was not as fast as some smaller dinosaurs.

58
00:04:49.937 --> 00:04:52.218
Scientists have also carefully examined the T.

59
00:04:52.278 --> 00:04:53.299
rex's forearms.

60
00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.043
The arms were quite short compared to its massive body, and had two fingers attached.

61
00:05:00.104 --> 00:05:03.686
These short arms might have been long enough for the dinosaur to grab prey.

62
00:05:04.146 --> 00:05:06.608
But they were definitely too short to reach its mouth.

63
00:05:07.429 --> 00:05:10.351
So it's been difficult to figure out exactly how the T.

64
00:05:10.431 --> 00:05:12.152
rex used these small limbs.

65
00:05:13.088 --> 00:05:21.492
Today, over one hundred years after Barnum Brown's exciting discoveries in Montana, paleontologists have more sophisticated tools for

66
00:05:21.512 --> 00:05:23.533
the excavation and study of fossils.

67
00:05:24.373 --> 00:05:25.454
But when it comes to the T.

68
00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:28.795
rex, they still have many more questions than answers.

69
00:05:29.236 --> 00:05:38.220
Perhaps future fossil excavations will reveal more about this amazing and massive creature—Tyrannosaurus rex, the tyrant lizard king.
